{"analysis":"Version 1 overcorrected toward neutral: 59 positive and 49 negative tweets went to neutral. Neutral errors fell, but the gain is smaller than the losses.

Positive tweets the model called neutral fall into three groups. First, friendly promotions and invitations ('See you there!', 'come check it out!', 'Don't miss...', tickets at a 'good price'). Second, personal plans for fun things (Disneyland, homecoming 'should be fun', Dunkin). Third, playful or affectionate chatter with :) or <3.

Negative tweets the model called neutral are mostly mild complaints and frustrations ('Argh', '#boring', 'stuck in the airport', 'poor decision', 'I'm a loser'). Sarcasm marked with #not and dismissive opinions ('most plain wrestler', 'Don't play Nicki') also went to neutral.

Neutral remains right for these cases:
- dry headlines, scores and statistics;
- legal, business or political news without a stated stance;
- tactical sports talk;
- plain questions.

Some bad-news headlines (an arrest, an oil-price crash, Charlie Hebdo) are neutral, so only violence or tragedy should make news negative.

Fix: neutral becomes the label for tweets with no personal affect at all. Any friendly, eager or fun tone leans positive, and any gripe, annoyance or sarcastic dig leans negative. Keep 'only clear celebration or tragedy' for news.","question":"Does the tweet carry a positive, negative, or no clear feeling, either in the author's tone or in what it reports?","rule":"Neutral is for tweets with no personal affect: dry headlines, scores and stats, legal, business or political news, schedules, plain questions, tactical analysis. Any friendly or eager tone (invitations with 'come', 'see you there', fun plans, :), playful banter) leans positive. Any gripe, annoyance, sarcastic dig, boredom or dismissive opinion leans negative, even if mild. For news without opinion, use positive only for clear achievements or celebrations and negative only for violence, deaths or disasters.","criteria":{"positive":{"what":"Enthusiasm, affection, praise, hope, fun plans, friendly promotions or invitations, playful chat, or reports of clear achievements or celebrations.","examples":["Looking forward to facing Ms. Allysin Kay this Saturday in Petrolia!!  come check it out!","@user headed to Baylor homecoming tomorrow with my baber. Should be fun even without RG3!","Tomorrow @ 5pm in GSC buy tickets to Ed Sheeran in concert at Riverbend on 9/17. Tickets are $20 cash. See you there!"],"cues":"see you there, come check it out, looking forward, should be fun, can't wait, love, good price, :), <3, Happy [day], celebrate","not_for":"Dry score lines, box-office figures or stat headlines without praise are neutral."},"neutral":{"what":"Factual or detached tweets: headlines, stats, legal or political news, schedules, plain questions, tactical sports talk.","examples":["Thursday's top stories including the latest in the lawsuit against Rolling Stone and Sabrina Erdely",""Yanks crush Sox with 3 HRs, 8-run 2nd inning: Associated PressBoston Red Sox right fielder Jackie Bradley Jr. ..."],"cues":"(Reuters), reports, said Monday, stats, 'who is', 'what do you think', dates and times only","not_for":"Friendly invites or fun plans are positive; any complaint, sarcasm or annoyance is negative."},"negative":{"what":"Complaints, frustration, boredom, disappointment, sarcasm, insults, dismissive opinions, or reports of violence, deaths or disasters.","examples":[""For the 3rd year in a row,I total spaced and missed International Talk Like a Pirate Day...Argh"","A Thursday night with no Waterloo road on! #boring",""I'm sorry, but Randy Orton is the most plain wrestler on the roster. He does nothing for me in the main event spot. 2nd is Sheamus.""],"cues":"argh, #boring, #not, stuck, missed, poor decision, loser, c'mon, does nothing for me, damn, killed, attack","not_for":"Legal or political news without opinion is neutral; strong words used in excitement are positive."}}}

{"analysis":"Version 4 (72.1) is the best so far. Positive recall (162 of 199) and negative recall (175 of 197) are high. Neutral recall collapsed to 101 of 204, with 62 neutral tweets going to negative and 41 to positive. The fix should restore neutral for specific tweet types without losing the polar gains.

Neutral tweets the model called negative fall into four types:
- tactical second-guessing in sports ('should have started', 'why move', 'odd decisions', game recaps like 'Mariners fall');
- argumentative replies on politics or religion ('You do realize...', 'C'mon Huck', jabs at Gandhi or Muslims, 'Tony Blair is right');
- small inconveniences told lightly ('still waiting', 'they cancelled lmao', 'I'm done', 'HELL NO' at a price, envy with ;_;);
- news of arrests or court cases, and dark jokes.

Neutral tweets the model called positive fall into three types:
- event ads ('Don't miss', 'place to be', 'Check out');
- plans with hope ('I'm hoping it does the book justice');
- nostalgic or joking banter, and celebrity news with 'kind words'.

Version 5 keeps version 4's evaluative-word approach but adds explicit neutral carve-outs for these types. Negative needs a clear negative judgment word or real distress. Positive needs the author's own warmth or praise, and an event ad alone is not enough. I keep the gold-negative examples of criticism of people ('hypocritical', 'bitter') so negative recall holds.","question":"Does the tweet carry an upbeat evaluation, a critical or unhappy one, or no clear evaluation?","rule":"Polar labels need clear evaluative wording. Positive needs the author's own warmth or praise (good, love, can't wait, :) or <3 aimed at something) or clearly good news. Negative needs explicit negative judgment (hypocritical, bitter, worst, sucks, flop) or real distress, or harmful news. These stay neutral even with a slight lean: tactical sports second-guessing and game recaps, argumentative replies about politics or religion, small inconveniences told lightly, event ads ('don't miss', 'check out', 'place to be'), plans, nostalgic or joking banter, arrests or court news.","criteria":{"positive":{"what":"The author's own excitement, affection, praise or hope for something, warm sales pitches with praise words, or clear good news or achievements.","examples":["Jason Aldean&amp;Kenny Chesney tickets for sale. Concert is the 29th. Good seats for a really good price. Text me asap for more info!!","We always mess up when we get the chance to go up the table when Atleti or Barca mess up. PLEASE WIN TOMORROW :) @user"],"cues":"good, best, love, can't wait, so excited, perfect, :), <3, congrats, record, Happy [day]","not_for":"Event ads with only 'don't miss' or 'check out', plain plans, jokes and nostalgia are neutral."},"neutral":{"what":"Information, ads, plans, questions, banter, tactical opinions, political or religious debate replies, mild inconveniences.","examples":["David Price should not have been pitching in the 8th. They can't get a 6-outs from the bullpen? Yankees - Jays","@user You do realize that the 1st Amendment doesn't just apply to Christians, right?"","I could be going to digi tomorrow but I thought I was supposed to be seeing the girls but they cancelled lmao","Check out the Christmas trading times for the Eagle Farm Markets! Don't miss the Twilight Fresh Market on Wednesday!"],"cues":"should have, why, c'mon, you do realize, still waiting, lmao, don't miss, check out, who's going, hoping","not_for":"Explicit insults or harsh judgment words are negative; personal praise or excitement is positive."},"negative":{"what":"Explicit criticism or contempt of people or things, real frustration or sadness, sarcasm, or news of violence or harm.","examples":[""Caitlyn Jenner may not be ""down"" with gay marriage, but she should really understand that people are gonna see that as hypocritical."","@user I used to really like Rick Perry, just has become so bitter. He may be very sad. He should try to smile","A Thursday night with no Waterloo road on! #boring"],"cues":"hypocritical, bitter, flop, racist, worst, sucks, argh, #boring, #not, damn, attack, dead","not_for":"Second-guessing, debate replies and minor hassles are neutral; strong words used in excitement are positive."}}}

{"analysis":"Version 6 is the best so far at 72.7. The remaining errors are mostly neutral tweets: 52 went to negative and 38 to positive.

The neutral tweets called negative are mainly:
- reported conflict, arrests, debates or market news in headline form ('slams Trump', 'arrest of Nabeel Rajab', 'Oil Price Not Done Crashing');
- jokes and dark humor ('scream cheater', 'Batman put people in comas', 'Chuck Norris');
- mild preferences or quitting statements ('doesn't like bdubs', 'I'm done', 'HELL NO' at a price, envy).

The neutral tweets called positive are mainly:
- event ads and hype ('Don't miss the event!', 'place to be', 'COOL OUT goes down', 'Retweet if you're ready', 'keep voting');
- plans with hope ('I'm hoping it does the book justice');
- nostalgia ('oh my god' at an old iPod);
- celebrity human-interest news ('kind words', birthday).

I make small targeted additions rather than rework. Third-person headlines are neutral unless they report violence or death or clear achievements. Event hype without a praise word is neutral. Jokes and dark humor are neutral unless they attack someone. To stay under 400 words, I shorten the cue lists and keep version 6's examples, which anchor the polar gains.","question":"Does the tweet carry an upbeat evaluation, a critical or unhappy one, or no evaluation at all?","rule":"Look for the author's evaluative wording. Upbeat words or emoticons (good, best, fun, love, :), <3) with no complaint make it positive. Critical judgments, setbacks, disappointment (:(, bummed), annoyance or sarcastic attacks make it negative. Neutral: logistics, bare invitations, event hype without praise words ('don't miss', 'place to be', 'retweet if'), questions, stats, jokes, tactical sports second-guessing, laughed-off hassles, and third-person headlines (debates, arrests, markets) unless they report violence or deaths (negative) or clear achievements (positive).","criteria":{"positive":{"what":"Upbeat feeling or praise: excitement, affection, hope for a team, warm sales pitches, fun plans, achievements.","examples":["Jason Aldean&amp;Kenny Chesney tickets for sale. Concert is the 29th. Good seats for a really good price. Text me asap for more info!!","We always mess up when we get the chance to go up the table when Atleti or Barca mess up. PLEASE WIN TOMORROW :) @user"],"cues":"good, best, fun, can't wait, love, see you there, :), <3, Happy [day]","not_for":"Event ads, 'who's going?' and dry score lines are neutral."},"neutral":{"what":"No evaluation: logistics, ads, invitations, questions, stats, headlines, jokes, tactical sports talk, laughed-off hassles.","examples":["So .... Who's up for seeing Ant-Man with me on Saturday? 2:30pm showing. Let me know!","David Price should not have been pitching in the 8th. They can't get a 6-outs from the bullpen? Yankees - Jays","I could be going to digi tomorrow but I thought I was supposed to be seeing the girls but they cancelled lmao"],"cues":"who's going, don't miss, should have started, slams, reports, scores, lmao","not_for":"Praise or warmth makes it positive; an insult, harsh judgment, :( or real frustration makes it negative."},"negative":{"what":"Criticism, contempt, complaints, disappointment, setbacks, sarcastic attacks, sadness, or news of violence or deaths.","examples":[""Caitlyn Jenner may not be ""down"" with gay marriage, but she should really understand that people are gonna see that as hypocritical."","I'm slightly bummed the Foo Fighters are playing Friday night of ACL rather than Sunday. I'm pretty much not gonna care the other 2 days.","A Thursday night with no Waterloo road on! #boring"],"cues":"hypocritical, bitter, racist, you just hate, bummed, dang it, :(, argh, #boring, killed","not_for":"Strong words used in excitement are positive."}}}

{"analysis":"Version 8 (72.2) did slightly worse than version 6 (72.7). Adding 'omg' and excited exclamations to positive pulled a few more neutral tweets to positive (42 vs 38): 'oh my god' at an old iPod, 'Don't miss the event!', 'COOL OUT goes down'. The harmless-jokes clause did not reduce neutral-to-negative errors (48 vs 52). The differences between versions 4, 6 and 8 are within about half a point, so the remaining errors look like label noise or genuinely ambiguous tweets. Examples are 'Christian Ponder would be proud', 'Thank the Gods', political replies and dark jokes.

This is the final version, so I return to version 6, the best measured formulation. Its parts are:
- evaluative-word criteria;
- neutral for logistics, bare invitations, stats, dry headlines and court news;
- explicit neutral carve-outs for tactical sports second-guessing, game recaps and laughed-off hassles;
- mild disappointment markers (:(, bummed, dang it) as negative.

I keep version 6's wording, which was within the word limit.","question":"Does the tweet carry an upbeat evaluation, a critical or unhappy one, or no evaluation at all?","rule":"Look for evaluative wording. Upbeat words or emoticons (good, best, fun, nice, hope, :), <3) with no complaint make a tweet positive, even in promotions or plans. Critical judgments, personal setbacks or disappointment (:(, bummed, dang it), annoyance, sarcasm or harmful news make it negative. Neutral covers tweets without evaluation: logistics, bare 'who's going?' invitations, plain questions, stats, dry headlines, court news. Also neutral: pure tactical sports second-guessing, game recaps, and inconveniences laughed off ('lmao').","criteria":{"positive":{"what":"Any upbeat feeling or praise: excitement, affection, hope, warm promotions, fun plans, good news or achievements.","examples":["Jason Aldean&amp;Kenny Chesney tickets for sale. Concert is the 29th. Good seats for a really good price. Text me asap for more info!!","We always mess up when we get the chance to go up the table when Atleti or Barca mess up. PLEASE WIN TOMORROW :) @user"],"cues":"good, best, fun, perfect, can't wait, love, hope, see you there, :), <3, !!, Happy [day]","not_for":"Bare event listings, 'who's going with me?' and dry score lines are neutral."},"neutral":{"what":"No evaluation: logistics, schedules, plain questions or invitations, stats, dry news, tactical sports talk, laughed-off hassles.","examples":["So .... Who's up for seeing Ant-Man with me on Saturday? 2:30pm showing. Let me know!","David Price should not have been pitching in the 8th. They can't get a 6-outs from the bullpen? Yankees - Jays","I could be going to digi tomorrow but I thought I was supposed to be seeing the girls but they cancelled lmao"],"cues":"who's going, anyone wanna, should have started, why move, dates, times, reports, scores, lmao","not_for":"Add praise or warmth and it is positive; add an insult, harsh judgment, :( or real frustration and it is negative."},"negative":{"what":"Criticism, contempt, complaints, disappointment, setbacks, sarcasm, sadness, or news of violence or harm.","examples":[""Caitlyn Jenner may not be ""down"" with gay marriage, but she should really understand that people are gonna see that as hypocritical."","I'm slightly bummed the Foo Fighters are playing Friday night of ACL rather than Sunday. I'm pretty much not gonna care the other 2 days.","A Thursday night with no Waterloo road on! #boring"],"cues":"hypocritical, bitter, flop, racist, you just hate, can't be serious, bummed, dang it, :(, argh, #boring, attack","not_for":"Strong words used in excitement are positive."}}}

{"analysis":"Version 9 (72.8) is essentially version 6 again and scores the same, 72.7 to 72.8. It is the most stable and best formulation found. Every attempt to shift the neutral boundary in either direction traded errors one-for-one:
- broad neutral carve-outs (versions 3, 5 and 7) pulled positive and negative tweets into neutral;
- broad polar rules (versions 2, 4 and 8) pulled neutral tweets into the polar labels.

